# Azure Storage Public Access Misconfiguration (Find & Fix)

## Objective
Demonstrate a common real-world cloud security finding, a publicly exposed storage container, by deliberately creating the misconfiguration,
proving the exposure with a real unauthenticated request, then fixing and verifying the fix, all on live Azure infrastructure.

## Environment
| Component | Details |
|---|---|
| Platform | Microsoft Azure (free tier) |
| Service | Azure Storage Account (Blob Storage) |
| Resource Group | storage-audit-rg |
| Storage Account | sakshistoragetest01 |

## Steps

**1. Created a storage account and container**
A new storage account was created, with "Allow Blob public access" enabled at the account level (this is disabled by default on newer Azure accounts,
and had to be explicitly turned on to demonstrate the misconfiguration).

**2. Set the container to public access**
The container's access level was set to "Container", making any file inside it publicly readable by anyone with the URL, no login or credentials required.

![Container access level set to public](./public-access-setting.png)

**3. Uploaded a test file and confirmed public exposure**
A test file was uploaded, and its direct URL was opened in an incognito browser tab (no Azure login active), to simulate a random stranger on the internet finding
it.

![File contents loading with no authentication](./exposed-file-proof.png)

The file's contents loaded directly, with zero authentication, confirming the exposure was real and exploitable by anyone.

**4. Remediated the misconfiguration**
The container's access level was changed back to "Private (no anonymous access)".

![Container access level changed back to private](./fix-applied.png)

**5. Verified the fix**
The same URL was requested again from the same incognito browser tab.

```xml
<Error>
<Code>ResourceNotFound</Code>
<Message>The specified resource does not exist.</Message>
</Error>
```

![Access denied after fix](./fix-verified.png)

The file was no longer accessible, confirming the fix worked.

## Assessment
This demonstrates one of the most common real-world cloud misconfigurations, publicly exposed storage containers are a frequent cause of real data breaches, often
from a single setting being left on the wrong value. The exposure here required no attack technique at all, just knowing (or guessing) the URL, which is why this
class of misconfiguration is so consistently dangerous in practice.

## Recommendation
- Keep "Allow Blob public access" disabled at the account level by default (Azure's current default), and only enable it deliberately when a specific,
reviewed use case requires it
- Regularly audit container access levels across storage accounts, rather than assuming defaults are always correctly applied
- Consider using Azure Policy to prevent public access from being enabled on storage accounts entirely in a real organization, removing the possibility of this
misconfiguration happening by accident

## What I Learned
- Azure now disables public blob access by default at the account level, a real security improvement, this had to be explicitly enabled to demonstrate the older,
riskier configuration
- Proving exposure with an actual unauthenticated request (incognito browser, no login) is much stronger evidence than just showing a setting, it demonstrates real
impact, not just a configuration state
- The same before/after verification approach used in my local lab projects (confirm the problem, fix it, confirm the fix) applies directly to cloud security work
too, the tools change but the methodology doesn't
